  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe gradient of about 10-12mb remains across the state today, with \nthe core of highest winds at 1/2km across mainly the Sisseton hills \nregion of about 35-40kts. The surface low is still expected to lift \nout of Wyoming into western North Dakota, keeping us within the warm \nsector until a frontal passage shifts west to east across the state \nthis afternoon/evening.\n\nAs we are within the warm sector, a southwest wind component should \naid in mixing and drying. RRFS also shows the high clouds across the \narea early this morning shifting east for ample sunshine today. Cold \nadvection moving in around the western CWA about 18-21Z and then \nrapidly spreading east will likewise aid in mixing of a mild 850mb \nairmass before temperatures drop. Thus, expect to overachieve \ncompared to the NBM, and used a blend of the HRRR for finer details \nin hourly temperatures to better account for the wind shifts helping \nto enhance mixing and then the onset of cold advection. Likewise, \nused the HRRR to add fine details regarding winds and wind shifts \nfrom south to southwest to west/northwest. While minimum humidity is \nstill pretty marginal for Red Flag, went ahead and issued for Lyman \nand Buffalo where the Hot Dry Windy Index runs between 75th \npercentile on the lower range of ensembles, with a few poking above \nthe 95th percentile. Elsewhere across much of central South Dakota \nthe Grassland Fire Danger Index is in the Very High category for \ntoday. \n\nRRFS profiles also indicate some shallow convective instability \nwithin the cold advection regime with skinny CAPE between \n4-12kft so only about 8kft of thickness in which convection \nwould be limited. RRFS placement is near the Pierre area, while \nthe HRRR is up closer to the ND/SD state line. The RAP likewise \nhas some precipitation, so will increase POPs along the boundary\nand transition east with the frontal depiction of CAMS. \n\nAs for post frontal winds, pressure rises are only 5-7mb in 6 hours. \nCold advection  is not insignificant, with an airmass of about +8 to \n+10C being replaced by something closer to -3C to +2C. RRFS mixed \nwinds in BUFKIT do top out at 40 to 50kts across northwest South \nDakota. Will leave headiness in place across the northern tier of the \nstate where the NBM 90th percentile is up close to 45-50mph, \nthough only for Corson/Dewey and the Leola hills, and much more \nmarginal for the other counties. Farther south across central \nSouth Dakota, CAMS suggest frontal passage winds will be strong \nbut brief lasting less than 3 hours on account of weaker \npressure rises and a weaker thermal gradient. CAMS do indicate \nwinds across the northern tier of the state extending into the \nnorthern Sisseton hills region remain up around 40 mph through \nthe overnight as the gradient doesn't relax much during that \ntimeframe.\n\nFor the rest of the forecast, cooler temperatures which are actually \nnear-average.  The system for Friday/early Saturday continues to be \nless impressive with more of a subtle southwest flow shortwave \nlifting up across the region. NBM POPs continues to drop, now down \nto around only 30%. A clipper follows and then the pattern aloft \nbecomes rather chaotic, and thus low confidence on weather \nconditions for next week at this time.",
